\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Software Overview
\f1\b0 \
The project software is split into two parts: 
\f0\b Particle firmware
\f1\b0  running on the Photon, and a 
\f0\b web-based frontend dashboard
\f1\b0  that communicates with the Particle Cloud API.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 Firmware (C++)
\f1\b0 \
The firmware is written in C++ and flashed to the Particle Photon. It is organized around a main loop that continuously reads sensor input, updates motor output, checks for lap events, and handles cloud communication.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 Joystick & Motor Control
\f1\i0 \
The joystick's X and Y axis values are read through the Photon's analog input pins and mapped to a speed and direction value for each motor. These values are passed to the DRV8835 motor driver via PWM signals. The Y-axis controls forward and reverse throttle while the X-axis creates a speed differential between the left and right motors to produce turning.\

\f2\i Lap Detection & Timing
\f1\i0 \
The HC-SR04 ultrasonic sensor is polled continuously during a run. When the sensor detects a distance reading below a set threshold \'97 indicating the car has passed the start/finish marker \'97 the firmware records the current timestamp and calculates the elapsed time since the last lap trigger. A debounce delay prevents the same pass from being counted multiple times.\

\f2\i Velocity Estimation
\f1\i0 \
Since the car does not have wheel encoders, velocity is estimated by dividing a known track length constant (set in the code) by the recorded lap time. This gives an average speed per lap in meters per second, which is then converted to a more readable unit for display.\

\f2\i LED Feedback
\f1\i0 \
After each lap is recorded, the firmware compares the current lap time against the stored best lap time. It then drives the WS2812B LED strip accordingly \'97 green for a new best, red for a slower lap, and a cycling rainbow animation if an all-time record is broken. The LED state resets at the start of the next lap.\

\f2\i LCD Display
\f1\i0 \
The 16x2 LCD is updated after each lap to show the last lap time and estimated speed. During a run it displays a simple live timer so the driver can see elapsed time in real time.\

\f2\i Cloud Publishing
\f1\i0 \
At the end of each lap, the Photon calls 
\fs26 Particle.publish()
\fs24  to push a JSON-formatted string to the Particle Cloud containing the lap number, lap time in seconds, and estimated velocity. This event is named 
\fs26 lap_data
\fs24  and is picked up by the web dashboard via the Particle Cloud event stream.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 Web Dashboard (HTML / CSS / JavaScript)
\f1\b0 \
The frontend is a single-page web app that connects to the Particle Cloud API using the device's access token. It listens for 
\fs26 lap_data
\fs24  events in real time using the Particle event stream and updates the UI dynamically without requiring a page refresh.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

